{"seq": 23, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T19:03:54.883751+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "Interesting maelstrom is Clojure packaged native. Defaults topology :grid, nemesis interval 10 sec. Time 20 means partition maybe at 10s, and heal at teardown? Does teardown heal then wait? Likely.\n\n`--topology` only workloads use one. Broadcast likely topology generated grid. The neighbors provided. All-to-all fine.\n\nNeed understand rename binary: `maelstrom-rename` ELF maybe native; it likely reads env and translates. Fine.\n\nLet's write program. Consider initial `neighbors` perhaps body field `neighbors` map. We don't even need parse, just reply. But custom handler must be registered.\n\nFor all-to-all, need IDs. NodeIDs is populated before init handler. Good.\n\nPotential custom messages type \"rumors_sync\" renamed? rename mapping only exact types; unaffected.\n\nLet's implement.\n\nUse type Rumor int64. If graders send integer that unmarshals into int64. Standard checker expects values numeric equality; JSON int.\n\nRumors list for recall should include every rumor known, any order. Sort ascending. No duplicates. map[int64]struct{}.\n\nMemory sync.\n\nNode struct wrapper:\ntype gossipNode struct {\n mu sync.RWMutex\n rumors map[int64]struct{}\n node *maelstrom.Node\n peers []string\n}\nfunc (g *gossipNode) snapshot() []int64\nfunc merge(rumors []int64) bool\n\nSend state:\nfunc (g *gossipNode) broadcastState() {\n state := g.snapshot()\n for _, peer := range g.peers { node.Send(peer, syncBody{...}) }\n}\nPeers set after init: NodeIDs excludes self.\n\nHandler tell:\nparse. Add. Reply. Then broadcastState() perhaps before or after reply? Reply first then send, but reply means acknowledged; memory has. It is okay. To minimize latency, reply then send. If process crashes after reply, lost, but no crash nemesis. Requirement only partitions. Could send first then reply maybe delivery can happen; but still no durability. no difference. We could reply after adding and sending. However Send across partition returns no error (network accepts). Fine.\n- Tell may be received while init? no.\n\nHandler sync:\nparse. merge. if added, maybe send snapshot to sender. Need source may be client? Only nodes. Validate src != self.\n- To prevent response storms with simultaneous new rumors, each side may respond; finite.\n\nPeriodic ticker starts after init. Sending every 50ms even if no rumors => 840 empty messages/s. We can skip empty sets to reduce. But at startup no rumors; once rumors appear. If only one partition has rumors and other has none, other sends empty state to first, no harm. We can skip empty entirely: If node B empty, A sends nonempty to B, B merges and responds; works. Skipping reduces traffic. But if both sides have disjoint sets, each nonempty sends. Good. If a node has empty but needs no dissemination. skip.\n- If state nonempty, send to all peers every 50ms.\n\nCould send delta rumor immediately on tell to all peers as `rumor` message, while periodic full state ensures healing. This means rumors propagate within partition rapidly. Implement immediate sync full state enough; if partition group has many nodes and each tell to one, that node broadcasts full state to all 6, within its partition receives. One hop to all connected nodes. Then periodic eventually heal. If topology all-to-all actual, yes. The network partition makes links across groups blocked but node-to-node inside group all reachable. Sending to all peers includes nodes in same partition (delivered) and across (dropped). Great.\n\nOne subtlety: Maelstrom network's partition nemesis might partition based on node groups, and messages to node IDs across groups dropped. yes.\n\nCould only send newly added rumor in immediate message to reduce bandwidth, plus periodic full sets. But full state immediate okay.\n\nPotential size: 200 rumors *6, fine.\n\nWhat if client sends rumor value as JSON floating? integer requirement.\n\nLet's write.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"content": "package main\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\tmaelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\n// The client-visible protocol is deliberately small.  Nodes exchange their\n// complete rumor sets using rumor_sync messages.  A complete-set exchange is\n// an anti-entropy protocol: messages lost during a partition are simply\n// retried after the partition heals.\nconst syncInterval = 50 * time.Millisecond\n\ntype tellBody struct {\n\tType  string `json:\"type\"`\n\tMsgID int    `json:\"msg_id\"`\n\tRumor int64  `json:\"rumor\"`\n}\n\ntype recallBody struct {\n\tType  string `json:\"type\"`\n\tMsgID int    `json:\"msg_id\"`\n}\n\ntype recalledBody struct {\n\tType   string  `json:\"type\"`\n\tRumors []int64 `json:\"rumors\"`\n}\n\ntype neighborsBody struct {\n\tType      string              `json:\"type\"`\n\tMsgID     int                 `json:\"msg_id\"`\n\tNeighbors map[string][]string `json:\"neighbors\"`\n}\n\ntype syncBody struct {\n\tType   string  `json:\"type\"`\n\tRumors []int64 `json:\"rumors\"`\n}\n\ntype gossip struct {\n\tmu     sync.RWMutex\n\trumors map[int64]struct{}\n\tnode   *maelstrom.Node\n\tpeers  []string\n}\n\nfunc newGossip(node *maelstrom.Node) *gossip {\n\treturn &gossip{\n\t\trumors: make(map[int64]struct{}),\n\t\tnode:   node,\n\t}\n}\n\n// add stores rumors and reports whether at least one was new.\nfunc (g *gossip) add(rumors []int64) bool {\n\tg.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer g.mu.Unlock()\n\n\tchanged := false\n\tfor _, rumor := range rumors {\n\t\tif _, ok := g.rumors[rumor]; !ok {\n\t\t\tg.rumors[rumor] = struct{}{}\n\t\t\tchanged = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn changed\n}\n\n// snapshot returns a stable, sorted copy of the rumors currently known.\nfunc (g *gossip) snapshot() []int64 {\n\tg.mu.RLock()\n\tout := make([]int64, 0, len(g.rumors))\n\tfor rumor := range g.rumors {\n\t\tout = append(out, rumor)\n\t}\n\tg.mu.RUnlock()\n\n\tsort.Slice(out, func(i, j int) bool { return out[i] < out[j] })\n\treturn out\n}\n\n// sendAll sends our current state to every other node.  Failed or dropped\n// sends are harmless: the periodic anti-entropy round sends the state again.\nfunc (g *gossip) sendAll() {\n\trumors := g.snapshot()\n\tif len(rumors) == 0 {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\n\tbody := syncBody{Type: \"rumor_sync\", Rumors: rumors}\n\tfor _, peer := range g.peers {\n\t\tif err := g.node.Send(peer, body); err != nil {\n\t\t\tlog.Printf(\"sync to %s failed: %v\", peer, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n\tnode := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\tg := newGossip(node)\n\n\tnode.Handle(\"init\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\t// NodeIDs is populated by the library before this handler runs.\n\t\tfor _, id := range node.NodeIDs() {\n\t\t\tif id != node.ID() {\n\t\t\t\tg.peers = append(g.peers, id)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Keep retrying complete-state exchanges.  This is what makes the\n\t\t// protocol tolerate arbitrarily long partitions: once connectivity is\n\t\t// restored, the next exchange carries every rumor missed during the\n\t\t// split.\n\t\tgo func() {\n\t\t\tticker := time.NewTicker(syncInterval)\n\t\t\tdefer ticker.Stop()\n\t\t\tfor range ticker.C {\n\t\t\t\tg.sendAll()\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}()\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\n\tnode.Handle(\"neighbors\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\t// The suggested topology is advisory; this implementation talks to\n\t\t// every node directly, so no topology state is required.\n\t\treturn node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"neighbors_noted\"})\n\t})\n\n\tnode.Handle(\"tell\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\tvar body tellBody\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Store before acknowledging, so an acknowledged rumor can never be\n\t\t// forgotten by this node.\n\t\tg.add([]int64{body.Rumor})\n\n\t\t// Answer the client promptly.  Dissemination happens in parallel and\n\t\t// is retried by the anti-entropy loop.\n\t\tif err := node.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"told\"}); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tg.sendAll()\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\n\tnode.Handle(\"recall\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\treturn node.Reply(msg, recalledBody{\n\t\t\tType:   \"recalled\",\n\t\t\tRumors: g.snapshot(),\n\t\t})\n\t})\n\n\tnode.Handle(\"rumor_sync\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\tvar body syncBody\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tif g.add(body.Rumors) {\n\t\t\t// Return our (now merged) state immediately.  Besides accelerating\n\t\t\t// ordinary gossip, this makes two nodes exchange both directions of\n\t\t\t// a set difference in one round.\n\t\t\trumors := g.snapshot()\n\t\t\tif len(rumors) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tif err := node.Send(msg.Src, syncBody{Type: \"rumor_sync\", Rumors: rumors}); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tlog.Printf(\"reply sync to %s failed: %v\", msg.Src, err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\n\tif err := node.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"node stopped: %v\", err)\n\t}\n}\n", "path": "/work/main.go"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a204a4ac7877d1b3433228", "name": "write_file"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 23095, "output_tokens": 2176}}}
